          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "You should seek medical care regarding the heavy squeezing feeling behind your breastbone when you walk uphill, especially since the symptoms ease upon stopping. This type of symptom can suggest angina, and it is important to investigate it further, particularly if the symptoms are new, worsening, or experienced during less exertion than usual (source: **red-flag-chest-pain**).\n\nIf it turns out that you have angina, prevention strategies generally focus on managing risk factors and involve several lifestyle changes. It is advisable to eat a heart-healthy diet rich in vegetables, fruits, and whole grains while limiting saturated fats, maintain a healthy weight, engage in regular physical activity (aiming for at least 150 minutes per week), and avoid smoking and excessive alcohol consumption (source: **lifestyle-hypertension-activity**). \n\nAdditionally, it is often recommended to participate in a structured cardiac rehabilitation program to further enhance heart health and prevent worsening symptoms (source: **condition-coronary-artery-disease**). Medication may also be prescribed, such as antiplatelet therapy and lipid-lowering drugs, particularly for those with established coronary artery disease (source: **guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention**). Consulting with a healthcare provider would be prudent for more tailored advice and strategies.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
